<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syphax</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 202 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Syphax was king of the Masaesyli of western Numidia in the late 3rd century BCE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siga">Siga</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>. Courted by both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, he sided with Carthage after marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a>. Roman and Massylian forces defeated and captured him in 203 BCE, and he died in Roman captivity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syphax.jpg" alt="Syphax" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Syphax.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Syphax was king of the Masaesyli tribe of western Numidia, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, during the last quarter of the 3rd century BCE. His story is told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s Ab Urbe Condita. His territory extended from present-day Constantine to the Moulouya, and in some periods his influence reached from the Moulouya to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When war broke out again between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 218 BCE, Syphax was at first sympathetic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. In 213 BCE he made an alliance with them, and they sent Quintus Statorius as a military adviser to train his troops. He then attacked the Massylians of eastern Numidia, ruled by King Gala, who were allied to Carthage. When Gala died in 206 BCE and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oezalces">Oezalces</a> quarrelled over the inheritance, Syphax conquered considerable parts of the eastern kingdom.</p><p>After Publius Cornelius Scipio won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilipa">Battle of Ilipa</a> in 206 BCE, he sent his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-laelius">Gaius Laelius</a> to ratify the treaty with Syphax. Syphax refused to ratify any treaty except with Scipio himself, so Scipio sailed to Numidia with two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a>. He reached the harbour at the same moment as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a>, who had fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Syphax invited both men to dinner, where both were taken in by Scipio's charm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> meanwhile concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was winning and changed sides, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> found a new ally in Syphax. Hasdrubal Gisco sealed the alliance by offering his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a> in marriage, although she had been betrothed to Masinissa until 206 BCE.</p><p>The combined forces of Syphax and Gisco at first made Scipio abandon the siege of Utica. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-plains">Battle of the Great Plains</a> in 203 BCE, however, Scipio overcame them. While he concentrated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, Laelius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> followed Syphax to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the pursuit, Syphax's army threatened to desert as Laelius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> approached its line. He rode alone towards the Roman cavalry in an attempt to rally his men, but his badly wounded horse threw him, and Roman soldiers seized him and took him to Masinissa. His troops withdrew to the capital, which fell, and Masinissa claimed his kingdom. Syphax was handed to Scipio and taken back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a prisoner. He died at Tibur, modern Tivoli.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a> then married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>. Scipio, suspicious of her, demanded that she be taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to appear in his triumph. To spare her that humiliation, Masinissa sent her poison, and she killed herself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syphax” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late third century BCE the lands west of Carthage were held by two Berber peoples, the Massylii in the east and the larger Masaesyli in the west, whose king <b>Syphax</b> ruled from Siga and later from Cirta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the late 3rd century BCE they formed a kingdom ruled from Siga, near the mouth of the Tafna river, with a second capital at Cirta after <b>Syphax</b> conquered eastern lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/masaesyli">Masaesyli</a></li><li><blockquote>After Gaia's death the throne passed through Oezalces, Capussa and Lacumazes amid civil war, while <b>Syphax</b> of the Masaesyli seized Massylian lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/massylii">Massylii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aïn Témouchent, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Tivoli. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.3,-1.14&amp;year=-212" aria-label="Aïn Témouchent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Syphax"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4920 4547h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4920 4547h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.3,-1.14&amp;year=-212">What was here in 212 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
